{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Command Aaron</b> — what is written above it? לאשמה בה ('for it is a guilt-offering'). 'Command Aaron' — to say that they should be zealous in Torah and commandments, for oversight in study is accounted as intentional." "<b>Moses</b> — לאמר צו את ('saying: command'). ס\"ת (the adjacent letters spell) תורה צוה ('He commanded Torah') — that they should engage in Torah." "<b>This is the law of the burnt-offering</b> — ה (at the end of העול) is the עו\"לה (= עולה, burnt-offering). That is, everyone who engages in the law of the burnt-offering is as if he offered a burnt-offering. The sacrifices are called אשה (fire-offering) and the Torah is called אש (fire), as stated: 'Is not My word thus like fire?' מימינו אש דת למו ('from His right hand, a fiery law for them'). Letters: תלמוד. The sacrifices are called לחם (bread) — 'the bread of My offering'; and the Torah is called לחם (bread) — 'Come, partake of My bread.' Just as the world cannot exist without bread, so the world cannot exist without Torah."], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {2 "The phrase מימינו אש דת למו is quoted from Deuteronomy 33:2. The word אותיות (letters) before תלמוד appears to introduce a letter-based observation, but no specific letter calculation follows; translated as found."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-12T19:19:49.972637861Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}